menu "Fan Relays"

    config FAN_RELAY_SPEED1_GPIO
        int "Speed 1 (low) relay GPIO"
        range 0 21
        default 3
        help
            Relay that powers the low-speed tap of the fan motor.
            Only one speed relay is ever closed at a time, with FAN_RELAY_DEAD_TIME_MS between
            releasing one and closing the next. Wire the speed relays as an interlock chain as well
            (see README), so two motor taps can never be powered together even by a fault.

            Good ESP32-C3 pins for relays: GPIO0, 1, 3, 4, 5, 7, 10 (no internal pull-up at reset,
            so an active-high relay driver stays off while the chip boots). Avoid the strapping pins
            GPIO2/8/9, GPIO6 (pull-up at reset), the flash pins GPIO11-17, USB GPIO18/19 and
            GPIO20/21 (UART0: the ROM prints on GPIO21 at every boot).

    config FAN_RELAY_SPEED2_GPIO
        int "Speed 2 (medium) relay GPIO"
        range 0 21
        default 4

    config FAN_RELAY_SPEED3_GPIO
        int "Speed 3 (high) relay GPIO"
        range 0 21
        default 5

    config FAN_RELAY_SWING_GPIO
        int "Oscillation (swing motor) relay GPIO"
        range 0 21
        default 7
        help
            Relay that powers the oscillation (swing) motor. It is closed only while the fan runs
            and oscillation is on; a speed change does not interrupt it.

    config FAN_RELAY_ACTIVE_LEVEL
        int "Relay active level (1 = active-high, 0 = active-low)"
        range 0 1
        default 1
        help
            Level written to a relay GPIO to close the relay. Many cheap optocoupled relay boards
            are active-low: set 0 for those.

    config FAN_RELAY_DEAD_TIME_MS
        int "Dead time between two speed relays (ms)"
        range 50 2000
        default 200
        help
            When the speed changes, the old speed relay is released and the new one is closed only
            after this time, so the contact of the old relay has opened (and its arc is gone) first.

endmenu

menu "Fan Behavior"

    choice FAN_POWER_ON_STATE
        prompt "After a power cut"
        default FAN_POWER_ON_RESTORE
        help
            State of the fan when mains power comes back. Software restarts (OTA update, factory
            reset, crash) always restore the last state, except after a panic, watchdog or brownout
            reset that hit a start younger than 30 s (then the fan starts off).

        config FAN_POWER_ON_RESTORE
            bool "Restore the last state (speed, oscillation, natural wind)"

        config FAN_POWER_ON_OFF
            bool "Stay off (speed, oscillation and natural wind are kept for the next On)"

    endchoice

    config FAN_ON_RESUMES_LAST_SPEED
        bool "Turning the fan on from an app resumes the last speed"
        default y
        help
            A controller that turns a stopped fan on without choosing a speed gets the speed (or the
            natural wind) the fan had before it was turned off, like the power button of the remote,
            instead of the speed 3 (High) that the Matter specification asks for:
            - FanMode = On (or Smart without Auto),
            - PercentSetting = 100 % while the fan is off: Apple Home turns a fan on that way (in
              HomeKit a stopped fan has speed 0, so "on" sends 100 %).
            So dragging the slider of a stopped fan straight to 100 % also resumes the last speed;
            once the fan runs, 100 % is speed 3. FanMode = On on a running fan changes nothing
            (natural wind ends). n: FanMode On and 100 % always run speed 3.

    config FAN_MATTER_ROCKING
        bool "Oscillation as Matter Rocking (Apple Home 'Oscillate')"
        default y
        help
            Fan Control Rocking feature (RockSupport = left-right). Apple Home shows it as an
            Oscillate switch, Home Assistant as Oscillate. Turns the swing relay on and off.
            It shows the oscillation setting, also while the fan is off: the swing relay itself
            only closes while the fan runs. n: oscillation only from the remote and the buttons.

    config FAN_MATTER_AUTO_NATURAL
        bool "Natural wind as Matter FanMode Auto (Apple Home 'Auto' switch)"
        default y
        help
            Fan Control Auto feature (FanModeSequence Off/Low/Med/High/Auto). Apple Home shows an Auto
            switch next to the speed slider, Home Assistant an "auto" preset: both turn natural wind
            on and off. Moving the slider (or any other speed setting) leaves natural wind.

    config FAN_MATTER_WIND_NATURAL
        bool "Natural wind as Matter Wind (Home Assistant 'natural_wind' preset)"
        default y
        help
            Fan Control Wind feature with WindSupport = natural wind. Home Assistant shows it as the
            "natural_wind" preset. Apple Home does not show Wind (use the Auto switch above).

    config FAN_NATURAL_MIN_S
        int "Natural wind: shortest time at one speed (s)"
        range 3 600
        default 8

    config FAN_NATURAL_MAX_S
        int "Natural wind: longest time at one speed (s)"
        range 3 600
        default 30
        help
            Natural wind switches between speeds 1, 2 and 3 at random, holding each one for a random
            time between the two limits (a gust at speed 3 at most halfway). With 8-30 s the speed
            changes about 200 times an hour (each speed relay switches 70-100 times an hour): prefer
            longer times, or relays rated for many operations, if natural wind runs many hours a day.

endmenu

menu "IR Receiver"

    config IR_RX_GPIO
        int "IR receiver (VS1838B / TSOP38238) output GPIO"
        range -1 21
        default 10
        help
            Data output of a 38 kHz IR receiver module, powered from 3.3 V. -1 disables the IR
            receiver. The remote buttons are learned on the device (triple click on the button),
            see README. Any IR protocol is accepted: NEC frames are decoded, other remotes are
            matched by the shape of their frames.

    config IR_LEARN_TIMEOUT_S
        int "IR learning: give up after this many seconds without input"
        range 10 300
        default 30

endmenu

menu "Buttons"

    config APP_BUTTON_GPIO
        int "Main button GPIO (active low)"
        range -1 21
        default 9
        help
            Click: fan on/off. Double click: open pairing (commissioning window). Triple click:
            learn the IR remote. Hold 5 s, then release: factory reset.
            GPIO9 is the BOOT button of ESP32-C3 boards; an external push button to GND can be
            wired in parallel (keep it released while powering up, or the chip enters download mode).
            -1 disables the button.

    config BUTTON_SPEED_GPIO
        int "Speed button GPIO (active low, -1 = none)"
        range -1 21
        default -1
        help
            Optional push button to GND: each click selects the next speed (1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1),
            turns the fan on when it is off. GPIO0 or GPIO1 are free on the ESP32-C3 SuperMini.

    config BUTTON_SWING_GPIO
        int "Oscillation button GPIO (active low, -1 = none)"
        range -1 21
        default -1
        help
            Optional push button to GND: each click toggles oscillation.

    config BUTTON_NATURAL_GPIO
        int "Natural wind button GPIO (active low, -1 = none)"
        range -1 21
        default -1
        help
            Optional push button to GND: each click turns natural wind on (and the fan on when it is
            off) or off (the fan keeps running at its manual speed).

endmenu

menu "Status LED"

    choice STATUS_LED_TYPE
        prompt "Status LED"
        default STATUS_LED_TYPE_GPIO
        help
            Blinks while pairing, for Matter Identify, while learning the IR remote and for
            factory reset; flashes briefly when an IR command is received.

        config STATUS_LED_TYPE_NONE
            bool "None"

        config STATUS_LED_TYPE_GPIO
            bool "Single-color LED on a GPIO (ESP32-C3 SuperMini blue LED)"

        config STATUS_LED_TYPE_WS2812
            bool "WS2812 RGB LED (ESP32-C3-DevKitM-1)"

    endchoice

    config STATUS_LED_GPIO
        int "Status LED GPIO"
        depends on !STATUS_LED_TYPE_NONE
        range 0 21
        default 8
        help
            ESP32-C3 SuperMini: blue LED on GPIO8 (active-low). ESP32-C3-DevKitM-1: WS2812 on GPIO8.

    config STATUS_LED_ACTIVE_LEVEL
        int "Status LED active level (1 = active-high, 0 = active-low)"
        depends on STATUS_LED_TYPE_GPIO
        range 0 1
        default 0

    config STATUS_LED_SHOW_RUNNING
        bool "Keep the LED dimly on while the fan runs"
        depends on !STATUS_LED_TYPE_NONE
        default n
        help
            Off by default so the LED stays dark at night.

endmenu

menu "Device Information"

    config DEVICE_VENDOR_NAME
        string "Manufacturer (Basic Information VendorName)"
        default "Haxeko"
        help
            Shown as Manufacturer in Apple Home. At most 32 characters (checked at build time).

    config DEVICE_PRODUCT_NAME
        string "Model (Basic Information ProductName)"
        default "Smart Fan"
        help
            Shown as Model in Apple Home. At most 32 characters (checked at build time).

    config DEVICE_DISPLAY_NAME
        string "Device name (Basic Information NodeLabel / ProductLabel)"
        default "Smart Fan"
        help
            Default name that controllers show when the device is added, and the name advertised
            before pairing (DNS-SD). At most 32 characters. It can be renamed in the app.

    config DEVICE_HARDWARE_VERSION_STRING
        string "Hardware version (Basic Information HardwareVersionString)"
        default "ESP32-C3 SuperMini"
        help
            Human-readable hardware revision, shown as "Hardware" in Home Assistant.
            At most 64 characters (checked at build time).

endmenu
